WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.247
Picture a small shop that opens at nine.

2
00:00:05.247 --> 00:00:11.996
Before the doors open, its program loads three lists: the price list, the stock counts and today's offers.

3
00:00:11.996 --> 00:00:14.352
Three threads load them side by side.

4
00:00:14.352 --> 00:00:18.300
A thread is one path of work, like one clerk in the back room.

5
00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:22.152
A fourth thread opens the doors, once all three lists are in.

6
00:00:22.267 --> 00:00:28.830
One morning, the doors open early, and the first customer buys a loaf of bread for 0.00.

7
00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.919
Great news for the customer.

8
00:00:30.919 --> 00:00:33.380
The price list was still loading.

9
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.507
The fix most of us try first is a nap.

10
00:00:36.507 --> 00:00:44.658
In the lesson's demo, the lists take 200, 300 and 400 milliseconds, and the door thread sleeps for 250.

11
00:00:44.658 --> 00:00:46.320
Will all three be in?

12
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:49.107
No.

13
00:00:49.107 --> 00:00:53.728
The doors opened at 271 milliseconds, with two lists still loading.

14
00:00:53.728 --> 00:00:59.523
As the clerks taught us in the first lesson, a sleep is a guess with a number on it.

15
00:00:59.633 --> 00:01:01.528
We don't want to wait for a time.

16
00:01:01.528 --> 00:01:03.423
We want to wait for three events.

17
00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:08.831
Java has four tools for waiting: a latch, a barrier, a semaphore and a phaser.

18
00:01:08.831 --> 00:01:11.547
So here's the question we're shy to ask.

19
00:01:11.547 --> 00:01:12.158
Why four?

20
00:01:12.158 --> 00:01:13.992
Isn't waiting just waiting?

21
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.934
The first is a gate with a counter on it.

22
00:01:16.934 --> 00:01:19.077
Java calls it a CountDownLatch.

23
00:01:19.077 --> 00:01:21.565
Ours starts at 3, one for each list.

24
00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:26.835
Each loading thread calls countDown when its list is in, and the counter drops by one.

25
00:01:26.835 --> 00:01:31.643
The door thread calls await, and waits at the gate until the counter reads zero.

26
00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:35.439
Price list at 228 milliseconds: two left.

27
00:01:35.439 --> 00:01:37.141
Offers at 328: one.

28
00:01:37.141 --> 00:01:39.470
Stock counts at 428: zero.

29
00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:42.694
The gate opens, and so do the doors.

30
00:01:42.694 --> 00:01:46.456
Not a moment early, and not a moment late.

31
00:01:46.567 --> 00:01:48.351
The counter only goes down.

32
00:01:48.351 --> 00:01:53.570
So a latch opens once and stays open, and a later await walks straight through.

33
00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:54.693
There's no reset.

34
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.763
But say a loader throws before it counts down.

35
00:01:57.763 --> 00:02:03.496
The counter stops at 1, and the door thread waits forever, with no error and no log line.

36
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:04.603
So, two habits.

37
00:02:04.603 --> 00:02:09.018
Count down in a finally block, which runs even when loading fails.

38
00:02:09.018 --> 00:02:14.436
And await with a deadline, say two seconds, so a load that hangs gets a clear no.

39
00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:16.265
Now the evening.

40
00:02:16.265 --> 00:02:21.321
The clerks add up the shop's three tills, one thread per till, one day at a time.

41
00:02:21.321 --> 00:02:27.063
Each day's three subtotals make the day's total, and nobody starts the next day before that.

42
00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:29.310
Let's try a latch of 3 again.

43
00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:30.197
Day 1 works.

44
00:02:30.197 --> 00:02:33.892
On day 2, thread 0 finishes first and calls await.

45
00:02:33.892 --> 00:02:35.961
Does it wait for the others?

46
00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:39.331
It doesn't.

47
00:02:39.331 --> 00:02:42.158
The latch is still open from yesterday.

48
00:02:42.158 --> 00:02:48.537
So thread 0 adds up day 2 with old numbers for two tills, and runs off into day 3 alone.

49
00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:52.972
We want a meeting point that closes again by itself, like a tour bus.

50
00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:58.899
At every stop, it leaves only when everyone is back on board, and the guide counts heads first.

51
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.270
That's a CyclicBarrier, built for 3.

52
00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:04.991
Each thread calls await when it's back on board, and waits.

53
00:03:04.991 --> 00:03:11.422
The head count is the barrier action: it runs once, on the last thread to arrive, and adds up the day.

54
00:03:11.422 --> 00:03:13.882
Then the door closes for the next stop.

55
00:03:13.882 --> 00:03:15.332
That's the cyclic part.

56
00:03:15.433 --> 00:03:19.799
The demo ran three days: 6,000, then 12,000, then 18,000.

57
00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:25.772
Till 2 closed every day, because it was the slowest, and always got back last.

58
00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.273
And a tourist who never comes back?

59
00:03:28.273 --> 00:03:30.036
A guide would go and look.

60
00:03:30.036 --> 00:03:31.460
A barrier just waits.

61
00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:36.884
On day 4, till 2 never arrives, and the other two wait at most 300 milliseconds.

62
00:03:36.884 --> 00:03:43.257
One gets a TimeoutException, which breaks the barrier, and the other a BrokenBarrierException.

63
00:03:43.257 --> 00:03:44.884
So give await a timeout.

64
00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:48.875
For a group that changes size, there's the Phaser.

65
00:03:48.875 --> 00:03:52.811
At the yearly stocktake, helper 2 only counts, then goes home.

66
00:03:52.811 --> 00:03:55.415
A barrier of 3 would wait for it forever.

67
00:03:55.415 --> 00:04:01.129
A phaser lets a party leave, like a guide crossing a name off the list: 3 parties, then 2.

68
00:04:01.833 --> 00:04:03.291
Last, paying by card.

69
00:04:03.291 --> 00:04:11.688
At the till, the bank checks each card over the network, about 120 milliseconds a call, and it allows five calls at once.

70
00:04:11.688 --> 00:04:16.893
On a busy Saturday, twenty customers pay together, and fifteen are refused.

71
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:21.809
Think of a car park with five spaces, and a sign that shows how many are free.

72
00:04:21.809 --> 00:04:26.062
A car that comes in takes a space, and one that leaves gives it back.

73
00:04:26.062 --> 00:04:28.775
At zero, the next car waits at the entrance.

74
00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:30.268
That's a Semaphore.

75
00:04:30.268 --> 00:04:32.501
The spaces are permits, here 5.

76
00:04:32.501 --> 00:04:34.517
acquire takes one, or waits.

77
00:04:34.517 --> 00:04:36.173
release gives one back.

78
00:04:36.173 --> 00:04:38.405
Each call to the bank is a car.

79
00:04:38.533 --> 00:04:42.509
Twenty calls through five permits, 120 milliseconds each.

80
00:04:42.509 --> 00:04:45.160
How many at once, and how long in all?

81
00:04:47.733 --> 00:04:52.199
At most five, and 502 milliseconds: four waves of five.

82
00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:55.982
Now a car that was never inside tells the sign it's leaving.

83
00:04:55.982 --> 00:04:57.455
What does the sign show?

84
00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:03.108
Six free spaces, in a car park built for five.

85
00:05:03.108 --> 00:05:05.180
Good luck parking in the sixth.

86
00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:09.056
Permits have no owner: release adds one, whoever calls it.

87
00:05:09.167 --> 00:05:15.108
So a stray release, say from a card check cancelled while it waited, quietly raises the limit.

88
00:05:15.108 --> 00:05:17.256
Five drifts to eight, then twelve.

89
00:05:17.256 --> 00:05:19.405
Release only the permits you took.

90
00:05:20.133 --> 00:05:24.179
The latch in code, Java on the left and Kotlin on the right.

91
00:05:24.179 --> 00:05:25.662
Count down in finally.

92
00:05:25.662 --> 00:05:28.089
Wait two seconds at most, then open.

93
00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:29.954
So, why four tools?

94
00:05:29.954 --> 00:05:32.566
Because each waits for something different.

95
00:05:32.566 --> 00:05:34.449
A latch waits for events, once.

96
00:05:34.449 --> 00:05:39.672
A barrier waits for the whole group, every round, and a phaser lets that group change.

97
00:05:39.672 --> 00:05:41.798
A semaphore waits for a free place.

98
00:05:41.798 --> 00:05:45.139
Ask what you're waiting for, and the tool picks itself.

99
00:05:45.267 --> 00:05:46.075
Quick check.

100
00:05:46.075 --> 00:05:52.675
Three helpers move stock every day for a week, and nobody starts a new day until all three finish.

101
00:05:52.675 --> 00:05:53.820
Latch or barrier?
